1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,930
Second normal form starts from first, then asks a narrower question.

2
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:11,222
If the primary key has more than one column, every non-key fact must depend on the whole key.

3
00:00:11,222 --> 00:00:12,569
Not on a piece of it.

4
00:00:12,569 --> 00:00:17,833
Enrollment has student_id and course_id as the key.

5
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:19,472
grade depends on both.

6
00:00:19,472 --> 00:00:25,930
Maya's A in Harbor Light is not Maya's grade in general, and not the course's grade in general.

7
00:00:25,930 --> 00:00:30,000
student_name depends only on student_id.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,861
That is a partial dependence.

9
00:00:31,861 --> 00:00:34,944
The name will repeat on every enrollment row.

10
00:00:34,944 --> 00:00:38,944
If Maya changes her name, you hunt every course she took.

11
00:00:38,944 --> 00:00:41,861
Move student_name to students.

12
00:00:41,861 --> 00:00:43,666
Leave grade on enrollment.

13
00:00:43,666 --> 00:00:47,527
course_title depends only on course_id.

14
00:00:47,527 --> 00:00:48,958
Move it to courses.

15
00:00:48,958 --> 00:00:50,722
The test is a sentence.

16
00:00:50,722 --> 00:00:54,805
Does this fact describe the pair, or only one of the partners?

17
00:00:54,805 --> 00:00:58,430
Facts about one partner belong on that partner's table.

18
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:03,472
If the primary key is a single column, partial dependence cannot happen.

19
00:01:03,472 --> 00:01:06,430
A single-column key is already whole.

20
00:01:06,430 --> 00:01:10,944
That is why people say second normal form is about composite keys.

21
00:01:10,944 --> 00:01:12,250
They are right enough.

22
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:14,708
You still want first normal form either way.

23
00:01:14,708 --> 00:01:20,444
A surrogate key on enrollment does not excuse stuffing student_name beside it.

24
00:01:20,444 --> 00:01:24,222
The real identifier of the fact might still be the pair.

25
00:01:24,222 --> 00:01:27,611
Look at the meaning, not only the columns you numbered.

26
00:01:27,611 --> 00:01:29,888
Repeating a name is the smell.

27
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:33,388
Updating a name in three places is the fire.

28
00:01:33,388 --> 00:01:35,236
Fix by splitting the table.

29
00:01:35,236 --> 00:01:38,125
Do not fix by asking people to be careful.

30
00:01:38,125 --> 00:01:40,763
Careful does not survive a second clerk.

31
00:01:40,763 --> 00:01:42,444
Draw the composite key.

32
00:01:42,444 --> 00:01:44,013
List the other columns.

33
00:01:44,013 --> 00:01:48,527
Arrow each one to the smallest set of key columns it describes.

34
00:01:48,527 --> 00:01:51,944
Arrows that stop short of the full key are the work.

35
00:01:51,944 --> 00:01:54,444
That drawing is second normal form.

36
00:01:54,444 --> 00:01:57,291
Partial dependence hides in shipping dumps.

37
00:01:57,291 --> 00:02:01,972
A file of lines includes customer email because the warehouse wanted it.

38
00:02:01,972 --> 00:02:04,861
Email depends on customer, not on the line.

39
00:02:04,861 --> 00:02:07,402
Put it back on customers, and join.

40
00:02:07,402 --> 00:02:13,208
If the dump is a snapshot for a truck that already left, that email might be history.

41
00:02:13,208 --> 00:02:18,138
Then it is denormalization with an owner, not second normal form failure.

42
00:02:18,138 --> 00:02:19,194
Say which.

43
00:02:19,194 --> 00:02:24,402
Wide composite keys collect extra columns the way pockets collect coins.

44
00:02:24,402 --> 00:02:28,486
Each coin gets the sentence: does this describe the whole key?

45
00:02:28,486 --> 00:02:32,277
If you cannot answer, you do not understand the column yet.

46
00:02:32,277 --> 00:02:34,013
Do not keep it until you do.

47
00:02:34,013 --> 00:02:39,777
Star schemas in warehouses sometimes sit in a different normal-form conversation.

48
00:02:39,777 --> 00:02:42,180
Dimensions repeat on purpose.

49
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:46,416
This lesson is about operational tables you update all day.

50
00:02:46,416 --> 00:02:51,319
Do not copy a warehouse shape into checkout because a talk mentioned stars.

51
00:02:51,319 --> 00:02:53,583
Checkout wants second normal form.

52
00:02:53,583 --> 00:02:55,152
The warehouse can wait.

53
00:02:55,152 --> 00:03:01,986
When a composite key is only there because you refused a surrogate, you can add the surrogate and UNIQUE the pair.

54
00:03:01,986 --> 00:03:05,222
Partial dependence is still about the pair's meaning.

55
00:03:05,222 --> 00:03:11,875
student_name still does not belong beside enrollment_id if it describes the student.

56
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:13,847
Meaning, then columns.

57
00:03:13,847 --> 00:03:18,652
The drawing of arrows from facts to the smallest key is the whole skill.

58
00:03:18,652 --> 00:03:21,291
Practice it on one ugly spreadsheet.

59
00:03:21,291 --> 00:03:24,513
The ugly ones teach more than the tidy bookstore.

60
00:03:24,513 --> 00:03:28,152
Ask of every extra column: whose fact is this?

61
00:03:28,152 --> 00:03:31,930
If the answer is only one partner of a pair, move it.

62
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:34,361
If the answer is the pair, keep it.

63
00:03:34,361 --> 00:03:37,680
If the answer is I do not know, do not load it yet.

64
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:41,375
Unknown ownership is how email lands on a line item.

65
00:03:41,375 --> 00:03:43,944
Known ownership is a two-table join.

66
00:03:43,944 --> 00:03:45,194
Prefer the join.

67
00:03:45,194 --> 00:03:48,000
The repeating name is not decoration.

68
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,277
It is a pointer you refused to draw.

69
00:03:50,277 --> 00:03:51,125
Draw it.

70
00:03:51,125 --> 00:03:55,291
Second normal form is that drawing, with less vocabulary.

71
00:03:55,291 --> 00:03:57,458
Use the vocabulary if you like.

72
00:03:57,458 --> 00:03:59,180
Use the drawing either way.

73
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:04,222
A table can have a key, types, and atomic cells, and still be wrong.

74
00:04:04,222 --> 00:04:07,958
Second normal form starts only after first is true.

75
00:04:07,958 --> 00:04:14,583
The simplified rule: every non-key column depends on the whole primary key, not a piece of it.

76
00:04:14,583 --> 00:04:17,666
Partial dependency is the name of the crime.

77
00:04:17,666 --> 00:04:21,055
It shows up when the primary key is composite.

78
00:04:21,055 --> 00:04:29,541
If the key is order_id plus product_id, product_name depends only on product_id.

79
00:04:29,541 --> 00:04:31,847
It does not need the order to be true.

80
00:04:31,847 --> 00:04:37,736
So product_name on that table will repeat on every order that sells the product.

81
00:04:37,850 --> 00:04:43,994
Update the name on one line, miss the others, and the catalog disagrees with itself.

82
00:04:43,994 --> 00:04:49,772
Insert a product you have not sold yet, and you cannot store the name without faking an order.

83
00:04:49,772 --> 00:04:55,244
Delete the last order line for a product, and you may lose the only copy of the name.

84
00:04:55,244 --> 00:05:00,383
Those are insert, update, and delete anomalies from a partial dependency.

85
00:05:00,383 --> 00:05:06,494
The fix is a product table with product_id as key and name as a column there.

86
00:05:06,494 --> 00:05:14,355
The order line keeps order_id, product_id, and quantity, which really does depend on both.

87
00:05:14,355 --> 00:05:17,175
Quantity is about this product on this order.

88
00:05:17,175 --> 00:05:18,869
Name is about the product.

89
00:05:18,869 --> 00:05:23,036
A non-key column is any column not in the primary key.

90
00:05:23,036 --> 00:05:30,897
If the key is a single column, partial dependency cannot happen, and second normal form is free once first is done.

91
00:05:30,897 --> 00:05:36,897
That is why surrogate keys make second look easy, and why composite keys make it visible.

92
00:05:36,897 --> 00:05:39,369
Easy is not the same as understood.

93
00:05:39,369 --> 00:05:43,688
Functional dependency means: if I know this, I know that.

94
00:05:43,688 --> 00:05:47,813
If I know product_id, I know product_name.

95
00:05:47,813 --> 00:05:52,619
If I know only order_id, I do not know product_name.

96
00:05:52,619 --> 00:05:56,730
Draw those arrows on a scrap of paper when the table feels off.

97
00:05:56,730 --> 00:06:02,911
Any arrow that starts from part of the key and lands on a non-key column is a split you still owe.

98
00:06:02,911 --> 00:06:09,466
The full definition talks about every candidate key, not only the primary key you happened to pick.

99
00:06:09,466 --> 00:06:15,425
If a leftover unique pair still has partial dependents, the table is not in second.

100
00:06:15,425 --> 00:06:22,147
For this lesson, hunt composite keys and leftover attributes of one parent hiding on the child.

101
00:06:22,147 --> 00:06:24,591
Move those attributes to the parent.

102
00:06:24,591 --> 00:06:26,688
Keep the attributes of the pairing.

103
00:06:26,688 --> 00:06:33,202
Then first still holds, and second holds, and third can talk about chains of non-key columns.

104
00:06:33,202 --> 00:06:44,758
Another composite: classroom assignment with key (student_id, course_id), columns student_name, course_title, term, grade.

105
00:06:44,758 --> 00:06:48,688
student_name depends on student_id only.

106
00:06:48,688 --> 00:06:52,341
course_title depends on course_id only.

107
00:06:52,341 --> 00:06:56,147
term and grade depend on both if a student can retake.

108
00:06:56,147 --> 00:06:58,161
Split students and courses.

109
00:06:58,161 --> 00:07:01,550
Keep enrollments as the pairing with term and grade.

110
00:07:01,550 --> 00:07:05,091
You just turned a first-normal-form sheet into second.

111
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:09,008
The full version of second talks about every candidate key.

112
00:07:09,008 --> 00:07:18,105
If UNIQUE (student_id, course_id, term) is the real identity, check dependents against that whole triple.

113
00:07:18,105 --> 00:07:22,063
Simplified second is the whole primary key you declared.

114
00:07:22,063 --> 00:07:29,772
Use simplified to learn, then glance at other uniques so you do not hide a partial dependency behind a surrogate.

115
00:07:29,772 --> 00:07:39,744
A surrogate enrollment_id as primary key can hide that course_title still depends only on course_id sitting on the same row.

116
00:07:39,744 --> 00:07:42,063
The surrogate did not grant second.

117
00:07:42,063 --> 00:07:46,161
The leftover course_title still fails the spirit.

118
00:07:46,161 --> 00:07:47,341
Move it anyway.

119
00:07:47,341 --> 00:07:50,980
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

120
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:54,036
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

121
00:07:54,036 --> 00:07:55,591
When does second apply?

122
00:07:55,591 --> 00:07:58,952
After first, especially on composite keys.

123
00:07:58,952 --> 00:08:00,758
What is partial dependency?

124
00:08:00,758 --> 00:08:04,563
A non-key column that depends on only part of the key.

125
00:08:04,563 --> 00:08:08,230
Where does product_name belong on an order line?

126
00:08:08,230 --> 00:08:12,452
On products, not on the line, if it is the catalog name.

127
00:08:12,452 --> 00:08:13,897
What stays on the line?

128
00:08:13,897 --> 00:08:18,230
Facts of the pairing: quantity, maybe sold price as history.

129
00:08:18,230 --> 00:08:19,619
Three anomalies?

130
00:08:19,619 --> 00:08:24,772
Insert, update, delete, from the leftover parent attributes.

131
00:08:24,772 --> 00:08:26,772
Single-column primary key?

132
00:08:26,772 --> 00:08:31,772
Partial dependency cannot happen; second is free once first holds.

133
00:08:31,772 --> 00:08:34,494
Does a surrogate automatically grant second?

134
00:08:34,494 --> 00:08:35,258
No.

135
00:08:35,258 --> 00:08:39,063
Leftover parent attributes still fail the spirit.

136
00:08:39,063 --> 00:08:41,091
What is a functional dependency?

137
00:08:41,091 --> 00:08:43,119
If I know this, I know that.

138
00:08:43,119 --> 00:08:45,494
Whole-key rule in one line?

139
00:08:45,494 --> 00:08:48,800
Non-key columns depend on the whole primary key.

140
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:50,161
Candidate keys?

141
00:08:50,161 --> 00:08:55,286
The full definition checks every candidate, not only the primary you picked.

142
00:08:55,286 --> 00:08:58,550
Classroom assignment leftover student_name?

143
00:08:58,550 --> 00:09:02,147
Depends only on student_id; move it.

144
00:09:02,147 --> 00:09:03,369
grade on the pairing?

145
00:09:03,369 --> 00:09:07,091
Depends on the whole enrollment key if retakes exist.

146
00:09:07,091 --> 00:09:08,147
How do you hunt?

147
00:09:08,147 --> 00:09:12,452
Composite keys and leftover parent attributes on the child.

148
00:09:12,452 --> 00:09:13,327
Fix?

149
00:09:13,327 --> 00:09:18,175
Parent table for the partial attributes, child keeps pairing facts.

150
00:09:18,175 --> 00:09:20,258
Why composites make it visible?

151
00:09:20,258 --> 00:09:23,758
You can see a key part that already determines a column.

152
00:09:23,758 --> 00:09:25,258
Why surrogates hide it?

153
00:09:25,370 --> 00:09:29,597
The key looks atomic while extra columns still describe a parent.

154
00:09:29,597 --> 00:09:30,861
Must first hold?

155
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:31,764
Yes.

156
00:09:31,764 --> 00:09:32,986
Nested dolls.

157
00:09:32,986 --> 00:09:35,292
Is line_total a partial?

158
00:09:35,292 --> 00:09:38,903
No if it needs both quantity and this line's price.

159
00:09:38,903 --> 00:09:41,472
Sold price versus catalog price?

160
00:09:41,472 --> 00:09:45,625
Catalog moves; sold price may be a freeze on the pairing.

161
00:09:45,625 --> 00:09:47,000
When is second done?

162
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,667
No arrows from a key part to a non-key leftover.

163
00:09:50,667 --> 00:09:55,389
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

